<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zande people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Azande</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zande, or Azande, are a people of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>. From the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avongara">Avongara</a> aristocracy built a series of conquest kingdoms among them, which resisted traders, the Mahdists and European colonisers into the early 20th century. Their beliefs in witchcraft and oracles became famous through Evans-Pritchard's study of 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zande-people.jpg" alt="Zande people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Atoroyo Sika · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Azande_king_and_the_Queen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
